// half_closed_channel — Two-pipe channel half-closure (caveat §2.3).
//
// The two-pipe channel architecture means send and recv touch DIFFERENT
// pipes (send → pipe 1 write_fd1@handle[4]; recv → pipe 2 read_fd2@handle[8]).
// Closing one direction (the parent's write end) leaves the surviving
// direction (child→parent, pipe 2) fully intact and operational.
//
// There is no dedicated `channel_close_send` builtin in VUMA — `channel_close`
// closes all 4 fds. To demonstrate a TRUE half-close (one direction only),
// we extract the parent's write_fd1 from the 16-byte handle buffer via
// `shared_memory_read_i32(ch, 4)` — a TYPED pointer-deref primitive that
// performs a native-endian i32 load (4 bytes) at handle offset 4, matching
// the i32 store `expand_channel_open` emitted at the same offset. The
// loaded i32 is zero-extended to i64. Using a typed i32 load (rather than
// `shared_memory_read`'s i64 load + `& 0xFFFFFFFF` bit-mask) makes the
// extraction endianness-agnostic: a native i32 load of a native i32 store
// round-trips the same value on LE and BE backends. We then close that
// single fd via the raw `syscall(57, fd)` intrinsic (asm-generic nr 57 =
// close, translated to native x86_64 nr 3 by the backend's
// syscall_abi::translate_or_warn).
//
// (See the F3-a root-cause report
// `scripts/audit/followup_wave3_big_endian_root_cause.md` for why the
// previous i64-load + bit-mask formulation extracted read_fd2 instead of
// write_fd1 on big-endian backends, killing the surviving direction.)
//
// Sequence:
//   1. Parent opens channel, spawns worker.
//   2. Parent sends 42 → child receives 42 (pipe 1 = parent→child).
//   3. Parent closes its write end (write_fd1) — pipe 1 write side closed.
//   4. Child sends 99 → parent receives 99 (pipe 2 = child→parent, intact).
//   5. Program exits 0 — the surviving direction succeeded.
//
// Expected exit code: 0
transform main() -> i32 {
    ch = channel_open<i32>();
    pid = spawn_worker();
    if pid == 0 {
        // Child: receive 42 from parent (pipe 1), then send 99 to parent
        // (pipe 2 — the surviving direction after parent's write-end close).
        x = channel_recv(ch);
        channel_send(ch, 99);
        channel_close(ch);
        return 0;
    }
    // Parent: send 42 to child.
    channel_send(ch, 42);

    // Half-close the parent's write direction: extract write_fd1 (handle[4])
    // via a typed native-endian i32 load — endianness-agnostic — and close
    // just that one fd. This leaves read_fd2 (handle[8], parent's recv end
    // of pipe 2) untouched.
    wfd: i64 = shared_memory_read_i32(ch, 4);
    syscall(57, wfd);

    // Surviving direction: parent receives 99 from child via pipe 2.
    // read_fd2 is still open, so channel_recv succeeds.
    y = channel_recv(ch);

    status = wait_worker(pid);
    channel_close(ch);

    if y == 99 { return 0; }
    return 1;
}
